; E170 lane E -- MUST NOT COMPILE. Sock: a receive of zero bytes.
;
; The wrong implementation: ask the socket for 0 bytes. `read(2)` with count 0
; returns 0 and is indistinguishable from end-of-stream, so a zero-length recv
; is a live bug class in C, not a made-up one -- and here the port's own type
; carries the bound: `sock-recv : (=> (1 s Sock) (refine I64 (> 0)) RecvR)`
; (ports/sock.chiral:69).
;
; ⚑ WHY THE WRONGNESS IS SEMANTIC AND NOT STRUCTURAL. The twin `pe-recv-1` in
; e170_port_twin.chiral is this file with ONE LITERAL changed, `1` for `0`. Both
; are `I64` literals; nothing structural separates them. What refuses this file
; is the REFINEMENT in the port's type deciding a proposition about a value --
; the only negative in the lane whose refusal is about the payload of a crossing
; rather than about custody of the capability.
;
; want: exit 1, `load: cannot prove refinement`

(import "ports/sock")

(def pe-recv-1 (=> (1 s Sock) RecvR)
  (lam (s) (sock-recv s 0)))

(def compile-main (-> I64 I64) (lam (n) 0))
